.TH "findlargedir" "1" "2026\-07\-22"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
findlargedir \- find blackhole directories with huge numbers of entries
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBfindlargedir\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIPATH\fR.\&.\&.\&
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBfindlargedir\fR scans each \fIPATH\fR for "blackhole" directories — directories
holding an extremely large number of entries in a single flat level, which
degrade filesystem performance and, on most Unix filesystems (ext3/ext4 among
them), \fBcannot shrink back\fR once grown.\& They typically arise from forgotten web
session stores, CMS cache or compiled\-template directories, or POSIX emulations
over object storage.\&
.PP
To avoid the very cost it is hunting for — a full \fBreaddir\fR(3) over a
multi\-million\-entry directory can wedge a process in uninterruptible sleep —
\fBfindlargedir\fR works in two phases:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP 1. 4
\fBCalibration.\&\fR It creates and then removes empty files on the target
filesystem to learn that filesystem'\&s bytes\-per\-entry inode growth ratio.\&
.IP 2. 4
\fBParallel walk.\&\fR It walks the tree with its own work\-stealing engine,
visiting directories only.\& A directory whose \fBestimated\fR entry count (from
its inode size and the calibrated ratio) exceeds the blacklist threshold is
reported and its subtree is \fBskipped unread\fR.\& Every directory that is read is
additionally reported on its \fBexact\fR live entry count, harvested for free
from the same enumeration the walk already performs.\&
.PD
.PP
Each \fIPATH\fR must be an existing directory.\& A \fIPATH\fR nested inside an earlier
\fIPATH\fR is skipped rather than scanned twice.\& By default symbolic links are
not followed and mount points are not crossed.\& The thread budget defaults to
the logical CPU count, with one thread reserved for coordination (see \fB\-x\fR).\&
.PP
Directories nested deeper than PATH_MAX are still traversed on Unix, by
descending component\-by\-component from the scan root's file descriptor.\&
Directories that cannot be stat'ed or opened (permissions, races) are counted
and summarised at the end of each scan.\&
.PP
Calibration writes to the target filesystem, so \fBfindlargedir\fR generally needs
to run as a user who can create files there (often \fBroot\fR).\& A read\-only or
unwritable filesystem is detected and has flagging disabled rather than
aborting the run; \fB\-i\fR skips calibration entirely.\&
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
.SS Traversal
.PP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-follow\-symlinks\fR
.RS 4
Follow symbolic links.\& Off by default.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-one\-filesystem\fR
.RS 4
Do not descend into directories on other filesystems (the default).\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-cross\-filesystem\fR
.RS 4
Cross mount points.\& Each filesystem encountered is calibrated separately,
since per\-entry inode geometry differs between filesystems.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Total thread budget (2.\&.\&=65535).\& One thread is reserved for
coordination (calibration, output, progress), so \fIN\fR threads spawn
\fIN\fR-1 walk workers.\& Default: the logical CPU count.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-skip\-path\fR \fIPATH\fR
.RS 4
Exclude \fIPATH\fR from scanning (repeatable).\& A skip path that is absent on
this host is accepted, so virtual or optional paths can be listed
unconditionally.\& Matching is literal after normalisation: with \fB\-f\fR, a
directory reached through a symbolic link is matched by the path it was
reached through, not by the link's target.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Detection thresholds
.PP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-alert\-threshold\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Report a directory whose entry count reaches \fIN\fR as a yellow alert, judged
on the \fBexact\fR count of every directory that is read.\& Default: \fI10000\fR.\&
Must be strictly less than the blacklist threshold.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-blacklist\-threshold\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Report a directory whose count reaches \fIN\fR as a red offender and \fBskip its
subtree unread\fR.\& The skip decision uses the pre\-read size estimate (an
upper bound, since a directory'\&s inode size never shrinks).\& Default:
\fI100000\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-accurate\fR
.RS 4
For blacklisted (skipped) directories only, perform an explicit entry count
instead of reporting the estimate, accepting the extra cost.\& Directories
that are descended are always reported on their exact count regardless.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Calibration
.PP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-calibration\-count\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Calibration batch size, raised to a 1000\-file minimum.\& Default: \fI100\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-calibration\-name\-length\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Length of the zero\-padded names used for calibration files (1.\&.\&=255),
matched to typical real entry names so the per\-entry cost is
representative.\& Default: \fI24\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-calibration\-path\fR \fIPATH\fR
.RS 4
Calibrate in \fIPATH\fR instead of the scan root (for example a writable
directory on the same filesystem).\& \fIPATH\fR must reside on the same
filesystem as the scan root; \fBfindlargedir\fR refuses to run otherwise,
since a ratio fitted on the wrong filesystem would misdrive the
subtree\-skip heuristic.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-size\-inode\-ratio\fR \fIN\fR
.RS 4
Skip calibration and use \fIN\fR bytes per entry directly (fixed overhead is
taken as zero).\& Overrides \fB\-t\fR; combining the two produces a warning.\&
Default: \fI0\fR (calibrate normally).\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Output
.PP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-updates\fR \fISECONDS\fR
.RS 4
Seconds between progress status updates; \fI0\fR disables them.\& Default: \fI20\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
.SS Information
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Print a short usage summary and exit.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
.RS 4
Print version information and exit.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Calibrate the filesystem and scan a single tree:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir /var/www
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scan as \fBroot\fR so calibration can write to the target filesystem:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
sudo findlargedir /
.fi
.RE
.PP
Skip calibration with a known bytes\-per\-entry ratio — the fastest mode, useful
when the ratio for this filesystem is already known:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-i 1024 /var/www
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cross mount points, calibrating each filesystem it reaches:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-m /
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exclude virtual and irrelevant trees from the scan:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-s /proc \-s /sys \-s /var/lib/docker /
.fi
.RE
.PP
Lower the alert threshold to surface smaller offenders, and report exact counts
even for blacklisted directories:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-A 1000 \-B 50000 \-a /srv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Calibrate in a writable scratch directory on the same filesystem when the scan
root itself is awkward to write into:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-t /srv/scratch /srv/objectstore
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run quietly, without periodic progress updates:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
findlargedir \-p 0 /data
.fi
.RE
.PP
.SH SIGNALS
.PP
\fBSIGINT\fR, \fBSIGTERM\fR
.RS 4
Set the shutdown flag: calibration and the walk stop at the next
opportunity (the next calibration file or directory entry) and the
program exits cleanly.\& A second registered signal (the same one
repeated, or the other) exits immediately with status 128+signo.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBSIGQUIT\fR
.RS 4
Not handled; the default action applies (terminate with a core dump).\&
Use it when the process is wedged and a core dump is wanted.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB0\fR
.RS 4
Success.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB1\fR
.RS 4
A runtime error occurred — for example the alert threshold was not strictly
less than the blacklist threshold, the \fB\-t\fR directory is on a different
filesystem than the scan root, or the interrupt handler could not be
installed.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB2\fR
.RS 4
Invalid command\-line usage — an unrecognized option, a bad numeric value, a
calibration name length outside 1.\&.\&=255, a thread count outside 2.\&.\&=65535,
a \fIPATH\fR that is not an existing directory, or no \fIPATH\fR at all.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH BUGS
.PP
A scan root whose own path exceeds PATH_MAX cannot be traversed (the anchor
for component\-wise descent cannot be opened); scan from a shorter path
instead.\& On platforms without O_PATH (macOS), that descent also requires
read permission on every transit directory; Linux needs none.\&
.PP
If \fBfindlargedir\fR is killed with SIGKILL during calibration, its temporary
directory (a name beginning with .tmp, holding up to 50,000 empty files) may
be left behind in the calibration directory.\&
.PP
Bugs and patches: <https://github.\&com/dkorunic/findlargedir>.\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
